messages.content:
Create a 90-second voice message script summarizing this classroom observation.

OBSERVATION DATA:
{
  "analysis": {
    "executive_summary": "E2E Driver used clear concrete models and everyday examples (cake, windows, colored counters) to introduce fractions and proper fractions, eliciting frequent student responses. Key growth area: formative feedback and differentiation — next time, try using the explicit feedback frame (\"Correct about X; next do Y\") for three student turns, add a quicker two-track written task (simpler counting sheet + challenge conversion task), and build 30–60 seconds of silent think time after a non-routine question to increase reasoning and individual accountability. Teacher reflection noted that pacing worked well and that she plans to give quieter students more time to answer, which supports implementing the wait-time recommendation and suggests readiness to trial small procedural changes in the next lesson.",
    "talk_time": {
      "analysis": "Teacher-dominated discourse: E2E Driver led the whole-class explanations, asked most questions, and supplied answers frequently. Students replied in short turns and choral counting; there were few extended student explanations, limiting opportunities to hear individual reasoning.",
      "student_percentage": 15,
      "teacher_percentage": 85
    },
    "questions": {
      "analysis": "Most questions were closed, seeking identification or recall (\"How many?\", \"What is this?\"). A few higher-order prompts asked students to judge whether a fraction is proper, but the teacher typically accepted quick answers without pausing for think time or asking students to justify their reasoning, reducing depth of evidence about student thinking.",
      "examples": [
        "Open: \"Why is 3/7 a proper fraction?\" (<time>-<time>)",
        "Closed: \"How many parts are there?\" (<time>-<time>)"
      ],
      "open_ended_count": 3,
      "closed_ended_count": 28
    },
    "goal1_formative_assessment": {
      "assessment": {
        "evidence": "Part 1 - What happened: E2E Driver uses frequent formative checks (asking 'how many parts?', counting partitions, asking which fractions are proper) and issues a short written task near the end (<time>-<time>) where students are told to write proper fractions for shown shapes. These checks are clearly aligned to the lesson aim (identifying parts, numerator/denominator, proper fractions), but most tasks ask students to identify or recall rather than perform reasoning or problem-solving. The written 'test' is whole-class and not differentiated; teacher checks understanding via rapid Q&A rather than diagnostic probing or error analysis.\nPart 2 - Quote: \"First tell me what is called the top number and what is called the bottom number?\"",
        "max_marks": 9,
        "timestamp": "<time>-<time>",
        "justification": "Why this score - what was observed: The assessments are aligned with learning objectives (LOs) and focus primarily on recall and identification (lower-order). There is some applied questioning (e.g., distinguishing proper fractions) but overall balance is toward recall/understanding, matching Level 2.",
        "computed_marks": 6,
        "competency_score": 2
      },
      "teachers_role": {
        "evidence": "Part 1 - What happened: Across the lesson E2E Driver leads instruction from the front, modeling shapes and shading, asking the class to count, and giving immediate confirmations. There is no evidence of the teacher circulating among students, listening to small-group work, or providing individual scaffolding. Even when students begin a written task (<time>onward), teacher remains directive from front, giving instructions rather than moving to coach individual students. Student responses are short and teacher largely accepts choral or volunteer answers.\nPart 2 - Quote: \"You have to write the proper fraction. Do it yourself — don't tell me, do it on your own.\"",
        "max_marks": 4,
    …(+43141)
